decisions:
  - key: 6675989a69c86659
    question: "Does the int-type validation reject any int subclass other than bool (e.g. an IntEnum or numpy integer), or is bool merely the one explicitly-called-out exception to an otherwise permissive int-subtype check?"
    options:
      - "Use a strict `type(n) is int` check, rejecting bool and every other subclass"
      - "Use `isinstance(n, int)` and special-case only bool, accepting other int subclasses"
    resolution: "Use a strict `type(n) is int` check, rejecting bool and every other subclass"
    rationale: "<why the team chose this; reviewed in the pull request>"

  - key: add396d22d1f352d
    question: "Does 'n must be an int' mean strict type(n) is int, or does it accept any int subclass other than bool (e.g. an IntEnum member or numpy.integer)?"
    options:
      - "Accept isinstance(n, int) and not isinstance(n, bool) \u2014 any int subclass besides bool is treated as a valid byte count"
      - "Require type(n) is int exactly \u2014 reject all int subclasses, including non-bool ones, with TypeError"
    resolution: "Accept isinstance(n, int) and not isinstance(n, bool) \u2014 any int subclass besides bool is treated as a valid byte count"
    rationale: "<why the team chose this; reviewed in the pull request>"

  - key: c4a3fd601d693a1f
    question: "Does the type check on n reject only bool specifically, or does it reject all int subclasses other than plain int (e.g. an IntEnum or a custom int subtype)?"
    options:
      - "Only exactly type(n) is int is accepted; any subclass (bool or otherwise) raises TypeError"
      - "Any isinstance(n, int) is accepted except bool is specifically special-cased to raise TypeError"
    resolution: "Only exactly type(n) is int is accepted; any subclass (bool or otherwise) raises TypeError"
    rationale: "<why the team chose this; reviewed in the pull request>"
